#!/bin/sh
# BeSpunky house setup — the chain feature. THIS SCRIPT INSTALLS NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
#
# Generated by @bespunky/nx-tools:devcontainer. Edit the template in the toolkit, not this copy.
#
#
# WHY THE FEATURE EXISTS
# ----------------------
# The devcontainer generator refuses to overwrite a `devcontainer.json` it did not write. When it meets a
# project that already declares its own `postCreateCommand`, the house provisioning script cannot take the
# canonical `.devcontainer/post-create.sh` name — something else owns that lifecycle slot — so it is written
# BESIDE it as `.devcontainer/post-create.bespunky.sh`, and the generator prints the one line a human would
# need to chain it.
#
# Nobody places that line. So in every adopted project the entire house floor — workspace deps, the Claude
# Code plugin pre-install, the apt packages, Playwright, the Firebase JDK — silently never ran. Not "ran and
# failed": never ran, with a 300-line script sitting in the repo looking exactly like setup that happened.
#
# The spec closes it in one property. From containers.dev/implementors/features/: a Feature may declare
# `postCreateCommand` (and the other four lifecycle hooks), and "commands provided by Features are always
# executed _before_ any user-provided lifecycle commands" in `devcontainer.json`. ADDITIVE, not a
# replacement. So a feature can run the house script without touching, and without competing for, the
# project's own `postCreateCommand`. That is the entire mechanism, and it is why this feature installs
# nothing: it does not need a build step to do its job.
#
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL
# ---------------------------
# Because the spec requires it. A Feature is "a minimum of two files": `devcontainer-feature.json` and
# `install.sh`. It is not a convention that can be skipped — the CLI generates a build layer that does
# `cd <feature-dir> && chmod +x ./install.sh && ./install.sh`, unconditionally and with no existence check,
# so a feature folder without this file fails the container build at `chmod`. Hence: present, trivial, and
# explained, rather than absent and mysterious.
#
# (The CLI chmods it itself, so this file does not need a committed executable bit — which is what lets it
# ship through the Nx `Tree`, and through the toolkit as a `.tpl`.)
#
#
# WHY THE COMMAND IS GUARDED
# --------------------------
# `postCreateCommand` in `devcontainer-feature.json` reads:
#
#   if [ -f .devcontainer/post-create.bespunky.sh ]; then bash .devcontainer/post-create.bespunky.sh || …; fi
#
# EXACTLY ONE of the two house script paths ever exists (see `writePostCreate` in the generator): the owned
# path writes `.devcontainer/post-create.sh` and the adopted path writes `.devcontainer/post-create.bespunky.sh`.
# So the guard tests the beside-path and nothing else. In an OWNED project that file is absent, the `if` is
# false, and the feature is a silent no-op — which is required, because there the house `devcontainer.json`
# already runs `bash .devcontainer/post-create.sh` from its own `postCreateCommand`, and running the same
# provisioning twice per container create is not idempotence, it is a second apt transaction and a second
# `install`. In an ADOPTED project that file is the only copy there is, and this is what runs it.
#
# The path is RELATIVE because Feature lifecycle commands are "executed from the context of the project
# workspace folder" — the same cwd `post-create.sh` already assumes (`WS="$(pwd)"`). An absolute path would
# have to guess the workspace mount point, which varies per project.
#
# `/bin/sh` syntax throughout — `[ -f … ]`, `if/then/fi`, `||`. The shell that runs a string lifecycle
# command is not promised to be bash, and the guard has no reason to need one. (The script it invokes IS
# bash — hence `bash <path>` rather than `./<path>`, which also sidesteps the missing executable bit on a
# file the Nx `Tree` wrote.)
#
#
# WHY A FAILURE WARNS INSTEAD OF FAILING THE BUILD
# ------------------------------------------------
# Deliberate, and it is the one place this feature disagrees with the owned path — where
# `devcontainer.json`'s `"postCreateCommand": "bash .devcontainer/post-create.sh"` propagates the failure and
# the create fails. The asymmetry is real and specific:
#
#   1. FEATURE COMMANDS RUN FIRST, AND BLOCK. A non-zero exit here aborts the lifecycle, so the PROJECT'S OWN
#      `postCreateCommand` — the one that was already there, working, before anyone adopted the house layer —
#      never runs. A guest that can prevent the host's setup from running is not additive. In the owned case
#      there is no other command to preempt, so failing loudly costs nothing; here it costs someone else's
#      container.
#   2. A `postCreateCommand` FAILURE IS NOT STICKY. Unlike an `install.sh`, which runs once inside a
#      content-addressed build layer and is a cache hit forever after, this runs on EVERY container create.
#      A fixed script is picked up by the next rebuild with nothing to invalidate — and the script is
#      re-runnable by hand at any moment (`bash .devcontainer/post-create.bespunky.sh`), which is already the
#      documented recovery elsewhere in the house tooling.
#   3. IT IS NOT SILENT. The failure lands in the post-create output the developer is watching, under the
#      script's own error, and the warning names the file, says setup is INCOMPLETE, and gives the exact
#      command to re-run. Swallowing without saying so would be the unacceptable half of this; the warning is
#      the half that makes it acceptable.
#
# The exit code is not echoed back. Carrying `$?` through would force double quotes into the JSON string and
# the escaping that comes with them, to restate a number the failing script has already printed its own error
# about, one line above.
#
#
# So: nothing to install.
exit 0
